# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE CONSTITUTION — Constitutional Core (nibli KR)
# Revision of utopia.nibli (v0.1). Epigraph unchanged:
# நான் வீழ்வேனென்று நினைத்தாயோ? — "Did you think I would fall?"
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# REGISTER: commentary here is deliberately flat and technical so the KR
# diff against v0.1 is legible. The manifesto voice is the author's to
# re-weave — I am not ghost-writing it. Provenance tags:
#   [v0.1]          — KR line unchanged from utopia.nibli
#   [v0.2 NEW]      — new rule or fact
#   [v0.2 REPLACES] — supersedes a v0.1 rule. The old rule must be DELETED,
#                     not merely supplemented: derivation is monotone per
#                     stratum, so an addition can only WIDEN what derives.
#                     A permissive rule left in place keeps its exploit.
#                     (Restricting amendments must repeal — a governance
#                     lesson the logic itself enforces.)
#
# Three exploits in v0.1 were confirmed against the native engine before
# this revision (each one asserted-fact away from total bypass):
#   E1  show($w,$o) -> defend($w): any defendant asserts show(self, anyone)
#       — even their own victim — and becomes unjailable under Art 6.
#   E2  false(X) never gates the multi-sig: a voided auditor with a live
#       Review credential still co-signs voids. Unfixable in-snapshot:
#       adding ~false to a false-headed rule is a negative self-cycle and
#       the stratifier rightly rejects it. Fix requires the epoch split.
#   E3  severe is only consulted when family: a severe non-domestic
#       offender with an asserted home fact gets the soft cage — the
#       farmhouse exploit the v0.1 commentary mocks. "Domestic harm blocks
#       the soft cage" was commentary, not KR: nothing derived a breach.
# All three are closed below; each closure has an adversarial pin.
# Predicate names are drawn from the committed alias corpus (fail-closed
# lexicon): hence authority / rotten / fit / err rather than
# official / voided / eligible / breach.

# ─── Article 0a [v0.9 NEW]: The record is closed ──────────────────
# Chapter 1 opens by saying that when somebody wants to write something
# about you that is not on the list, "they cannot. Not *may not*.
# Cannot." **Until this block that sentence was false.** nibli's lexicon
# is fail-closed, so an invented word like `zorblat(Adam).` was always a
# compile error — but `rich(Adam).` loaded and answered TRUE, because
# `rich` is a corpus name. The closure was at nibli's vocabulary of some
# thousands, not at this file's two dozen.
#
# `admits` closes it here. An unadmitted ground assertion is refused at
# assert time with a message that names the repair: add the `admits` line
# ABOVE the first use. That is the point — widening the record becomes a
# visible, reviewable edit instead of a fact somebody types.
#
# ORDERING IS ENFORCED, not merely documented: an `admits` placed below
# the facts is refused as "comes too late", because a late declaration
# would silently grandfather everything above it. Same hazard as Article
# 0's, and unlike Article 0 the engine catches this one.
#
# EXTENSIONAL ONLY. This closes what may be WRITTEN. Rules still derive
# `false`, `prisoner`, `err`, `obliged` and the rest — closing the record
# is not closing the conclusions, and Article 0 is what does the latter.
#
# WRITABLE NAMES AND EVIDENCE PREDICATES ARE DIFFERENT SETS.
# The generated spine counts predicates with no producing rule; this declaration
# block instead lists every relation that a ground record may write. `person`
# has producing rules as well as ground assertions, so it is absent from the
# first set and must still appear here or the file will not load. The temporal
# admissions below widen both sets without changing that distinction. Do not
# copy either count into commentary; the generator owns them.
# [v0.9 2026-08-02] `married` (speni) and `sibling` (tunba) joined both
# lists with the kinship ruling — guards on the multi-sig's independence
# check, dead until a marriage or siblinghood is recorded.
# [v0.9 2026-08-02] `put` (punji: who put whom where) joined both lists
# when the placement marker was repaired — the record can now hold a
# report of the design's own act of placing somebody, which is the fact
# the repaired marker checks against derived eligibility.
# **"What counts as evidence" and "what may be written" are different
# sets**, and `person` is the whole of the difference — which is exactly
# what chapter 1 says when it puts personhood on its list.
admits("adjust").     admits("attack").
admits("ratifies").   admits("endorses").
admits("broken").     admits("capture").    admits("choose").
admits("cite").       admits("clear").      admits("cruel").
admits("deceive").    admits("family").     admits("forgive").
admits("free").       admits("hears").      admits("home").
admits("injure").     admits("judge").      admits("married").
admits("parent").     admits("permanent").
admits("person").     admits("public").     admits("put").
admits("rotten").     admits("show").       admits("sibling").
admits("suggest").    admits("teaches").    admits("work").
# <UNIVERSAL-STANDING-ADMISSIONS-BEGIN>
# [v1.1] born and at are externally supplied roots. An at subject is only an
# encounter handle unless a separate consequential-record process authenticates
# a civil identity. Neither input supplies citizenship, capacity, family, guilt,
# liability, record ownership, or authority.
admits("at").         admits("born").
# <UNIVERSAL-STANDING-ADMISSIONS-END>
# <T1-ADMISSIONS-BEGIN>
# [v1.0 T1-T3] These narrow relations hold the reviewed temporal envelope.
# `list` and `replace` name snapshot lineage; `authorized`, `carries`, `limit`,
# `continue`, `date`, and `passport` separate status, carry, lease, renewal,
# boundary, and source claims; `observe` carries independently controlled
# attestations; `challenge` opens the subject-side contest route. They remain
# reported inputs, not proof that a writer, witness, or outside event is genuine.
admits("authorized"). admits("carries").    admits("challenge").
admits("continue").   admits("date").       admits("limit").
admits("list").       admits("observe").    admits("passport").
admits("replace").
# <T1-ADMISSIONS-END>

# ─── Article 0 [v0.3 NEW]: Closed relations (EDB/IDB separation) ───
# These relations may ONLY be reached by derivation. A direct assertion of
# any of them is refused by the engine at assert time, fail-closed:
#   "`fit` is declared derived-only"
# This is what makes the listed conclusion gates mean what they say. Before
# it, every gate this file called "derived" was one asserted fact from bypass —
# `fit(Ruk, Homestay).` silenced the farmhouse breach marker outright.
#
# ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING: the check fires at assert time, so a
# declaration protects only what is asserted AFTER it. These must stay at
# the TOP of the file, above the cast. A declaration moved below the facts
# it guards is inert and looks identical.
#
# Converted aliases are closed too — `permitted` compiles to the `permits`
# relation, so closing a relation closes every surface spelling of it.
derived_only("fit").
derived_only("defend").
derived_only("false").
derived_only("reward").
derived_only("become").
derived_only("err").
derived_only("authority").
derived_only("permits").
derived_only("prisoner").
derived_only("severe").
# [v0.9 2026-08-02] Three more, closing by declaration what was closed by
# omission. `entitled` and `owe` are the record of what is owed — the floor
# itself — and neither name is admitted, so forging that record was already
# refused, but by absence: later exact-name `admits` edits would have reopened
# them. `building` the same — every placement rule requires `prisoner`, and an
# asserted placement was refused only for want of an exact-name admits line.
# Verified before landing: the floor lines, the eight itemised debts and all
# three placements still derive, and the whole suite stays green against the
# declared copy.
derived_only("entitled").
derived_only("owe").
derived_only("building").
# <LIBERTY-ECOLOGY-DERIVED-BEGIN>
# [v1.2] These protections are conclusions, never supplied verdicts. The
# prevents relation records a constitutional barrier only; it does not operate
# a service, measure an ecosystem, authenticate a source, or execute a remedy.
derived_only("prevents").
# <LIBERTY-ECOLOGY-DERIVED-END>
# [v0.9 2026-08-02] `clean` is DERIVED absolution — forgive + the body's
# judgment produce it, nobody writes it. Hold it apart from `clear`, which
# is the ASSERTED half of appellate relief for convictions: clear is a
# word the world may say, clean is a conclusion the machinery reaches.
derived_only("clean").
# [v0.9 2026-08-04] L1 + D1 close two more exact-name assertion routes.
# `lose` is the rule-concluded legal loss marker, and legacy `decide` is the
# rule-concluded franchise status used with Ballot. Both used to be refused
# only because their exact names were absent from `admits`; adding the matching
# admission made either ground conclusion writable. These declarations reserve
# each relation at every arity and through any future converted alias, while
# leaving rule heads free to derive it. They do not give `lose` a reader, make
# `decide` evidence of a cast ballot or count, or complete the wider assertion-
# surface sweep: the eight floor actualities, `obliged`, and `travel` remain for
# their interface decisions and the generated census.
derived_only("lose").
derived_only("decide").
# [2026-08-17] M1 closes the same gap for `mature`, which is not merely
# unadmitted but gone: its franchise rule and its four cast facts were removed
# when general adulthood became typed supplied evidence. That left the name
# reserved by OMISSION from `admits` alone — exactly the state L1 and D1 were
# written to end for two other relations — so one re-added admission would make
# a retired legal status writable again. This reserves it at every arity and
# through any future converted alias. It does not resurrect the relation, give
# it a producer or a reader, or make adulthood assertable by another route:
# `at($x, GeneralAdulthood)` remains the only path, and nothing reads `~mature`.
derived_only("mature").
# <T1-DERIVED-BEGIN>
# [v1.0 T1-T3] Assurance conclusions cannot be supplied as facts. `complete`
# tags a witnessed evidence item and scope, `succeed` tags an accepted successor,
# `correct` tags an active case-bound lease, `collide` names a typed conflict,
# and `match` is a legally effective reconciled status. Consumers rejoin every
# exact raw field they use.
# None authenticates its own premises; the temporal assurance case owns that
# external boundary.
derived_only("complete").
derived_only("collide").
derived_only("correct").
derived_only("match").
derived_only("succeed").
# <T1-DERIVED-END>
# <T2-DERIVED-BEGIN>
# [v1.0 T2] Event order is derived only from a separately attested event claim
# and record-entry claim. `precede/2` tags the witnessed claim; `precede/3`
# closes its endpoint path so cycles longer than one direct reversal remain
# visible. An asserted `earlier` or tensed wrapper remains inert.
derived_only("precede").
# <T2-DERIVED-END>
# <T3-DERIVED-BEGIN>
# [v1.0 T3] A constitutional interval is a derived relation over independently
# reviewed boundary events. It is not a clock and does not prove advancement.
derived_only("time").
# Typed assurance conclusions keep independent input classes from becoming one
# large self-join. Their identifier-plus-tag heads certify a reviewed input
# family; every consumer must rejoin its case, subject, holder, limit, renewal,
# or source fields from the raw relations.
derived_only("concurrent").
derived_only("orderly").
derived_only("reference").
derived_only("related").
# <T3-DERIVED-END>
# [v0.5] severe was the tenth and last open gate, and it was open in exactly
# the way the v0.3 three were: a conclusion about an offence, written by
# whoever held the pen, wearing the shape of a reported fact. Before this,
# `severe(X).` asserted with nothing behind it routed X to high security and
# no rule could tell the difference. See Article 6.
# [v0.3] authority, permits and prisoner were the last three open, and each
# was open for the same reason: the article conflated a fact the world reports
# with a conclusion the system draws, under one relation name. Splitting them
# closed all three —
#   authority — institutions now assert `public(Court).` and authority derives.
#   permits   — relief asserts `clear(Nia).` beside the appeals judgment, and
#               permits(Appeals, ·) derives [v0.9: the bare flag stopped sufficing].
#   prisoner  — Adam is convicted through Article 6 rather than asserted.
# That is this book's own thesis applied to its own constitution: evidence is
# what the world reports, and a conclusion must be earned by a rule.

# ─── Article 1: The Unconditional Floor ───────────────────── [v0.1]
#
# [v0.3] THE FLOOR IS A COMPILE-TIME PROHIBITION, NOT A DECLARATION.
# This was missed through v0.2 and it is the strongest property in the
# file. `entitled(every person, event { P() })` does NOT assert an inert
# fact: it compiles to a rule with `person` in the body, so P becomes
# derived from person, and `prisoner -> person` below puts P downstream
# of prisoner. Any later rule taking ~P into that cone closes a negative
# cycle and the stratifier REJECTS it. Verified both directions:
#   without the believe line:  person($x) & ~believe($x) -> prisoner($x).
#                              => 0 errors. The heretic is jailed.
#   with it:                   => "Unstratifiable negation: strongly-
#                              connected component containing 'prisoner'
#                              -> 'believe' (negative)". Will not compile.
#   control (non-floor):       person($x) & ~home($x) -> prisoner($x).
#                              => 0 errors. So the floor line is doing it.
# So: you cannot write a law that punishes a person for LACKING a floor
# right. Not "may not" — cannot.
#
# WHERE THE FIREWALL STOPS (disclosed, all verified to load at 0 errors
# with the floor lines present — do not let prose overclaim this):
#   ~believe($x) -> false($x).            credibility can still be voided
#   ~meets($x) -> lose(Points, $x).       points can still be clawed back
#   prisoner($x) -> believe($x).          positive compulsion is untouched
# The floor blocks punishment for ABSENCE, never manufacture, and it
# reaches `prisoner` only — not credibility, not points.
#
# INVARIANT 1 — A FLOOR RIGHT MAY BE NOTICED AND MAY NOT BE ACTED ON.
# A floor predicate's name may appear in a rule body ONLY where the head
# is `err`. Anything else — a floor right feeding placement, credibility,
# recognition or conviction — is refused. Note this is about the
# predicate INSIDE the event, not the outer `entitled`. Rule HEADS are
# always safe (dwell and expresses are both). Verified: putting `choose`
# on the floor un-stratifies Article 8 and SILENTLY DROPS the Review-
# credential rule — the capture defence dies while the amendment engine
# keeps running.
#
# [v0.8] THIS WAS REWRITTEN BECAUSE THE OLD FORM WAS BROKEN AND NOBODY
# KNEW. It read "a floor predicate's name must appear in NO RULE BODY",
# and Article 6's isolation marker — `prisoner($p) & ~meets($p) ->
# err($p, Isolation)` — has violated it since v0.1. Nothing checked, so
# nothing said. The blanket ban was also the wrong shape: it forbade the
# design from NOTICING that a floor right had not arrived, which is the
# one thing the isolation marker exists to do and the only delivery check
# in the file. What is actually dangerous is a floor right reaching a
# consequence, and the narrowed form says exactly that. `verify.sh`
# enforces it now, which the old form never had.
#
# The distinction is load-bearing and it is thin. `~eats($x) -> err(...)`
# NOTICES. `~eats($x) -> prisoner($x)` PUNISHES. The stratifier refuses
# the second on its own — it is a negative cycle through the person cone
# — but it does NOT refuse `~eats($x) -> reward($x)` or a placement rule,
# and neither would be caught by anything except this invariant.
#
# INVARIANT 2 — THE SHAPE, and the one most likely to be broken by
# someone trying to HELP. The floor line must be exactly:
#     PRED(every person, event { P() }).
# a universal restricted by `person` in x1, and P inside an `event { }`
# abstraction in x2. Traced in the engine (nibli session, 2026-07-28):
# `event { P() }` compiles to `nu(absvar) & __abs_H(absvar) & P(ev2) & …`,
# and `flatten_consequent` (nibli-reason/src/rules.rs:388) descends BOTH
# `And` branches unconditionally, so the body's P lands in the rule head
# and becomes a dependency edge to the restrictor `person`. Meanwhile
# `collect_ground_facts` (rules.rs:1934) DOES honour `__abs_` opacity and
# skips the body. **The protection lives in that asymmetry.**
#
# Two consequences, both verified:
#   - The outer predicate name is FREE. `entitled` was chosen for meaning
#     alone: `obligated`, `support`, `serve` and `teaches` were all tested
#     and give identical protection. The predicate buys nothing
#     structural, so choose it purely for what it says.
#   - Drop the event and the protection dies. `entitled(every person,
#     Belief).` — a plain term in x2 — loads the heresy law at 0 errors.
# Also refuted, all behaving exactly like writing no floor line at all —
# the natural "name the duty-bearer" repair is STRUCTURALLY WORTHLESS:
#     obligated(State, event { believe(every person) }).
#     owe(State, Belief, every person).
#     all $x: person($x) -> owe(State, Belief, $x).
# If a predicate reads wrong, change the PREDICATE, never the shape.
#
# [v0.8] A THIRD REASON, and since nibli `de34cd4` it is the sharpest one.
# `event { P() }` takes NO argument, and threading the universal into it —
# `entitled(every person, event { eats($x) })` — is a damaging edit, and
# RE-MEASURED 2026-08-03 IT DAMAGES THE OPPOSITE THING FROM WHAT THIS
# COMMENT SAID FOR MONTHS. The old text claimed the threaded variable
# makes `eats(Adam)` derive for every person on the roster, faking
# delivery "while every entitlement pin stays green", and named the
# actuality pins as the guard. Measured on a copy carrying exactly that
# edit: `eats(Adam)` and `eats(Bela)` stay FALSE — no actuality is
# fabricated — and `entitled(Adam, event { eats() })` goes FALSE. The
# edit does not forge the delivery. IT DELETES THE PROMISE: the floor
# line stops entitling anyone while still sitting there looking like a
# floor. So the guard is real and it is the OTHER pins — the
# entitlement pins, which go red at once (measured: rights-floor
# :281/:320 and `08-what-you-are-owed.pins.nibli:174` all flip TRUE to
# FALSE). Whoever writes this edit is caught, immediately, by the pins
# that assert the floor still promises something. Do not restate the
# old symptom; it was wrong in the direction that flatters the design,
# which is the direction to distrust. Nothing checked this comment,
# which is why it rotted — the same failure mode as the spine that went
# stale while nobody noticed.
# The engine is faithful to whichever shape it is given. The opacity is a
# property of THIS file's shape, not a protection the engine supplies.
#
# INVARIANT 3 — ANY ABSTRACTION THAT REACHES A RULE HEAD JOINS THE
# PROTECTED SET. Corrected 2026-07-28: I first wrote this as "x3 must be
# empty", which named the wrong thing. The condition is **rule-head
# position**, not place index, not `entitled`, not whether the place is
# referenced by the predicate's template. Verified, control = the
# non-floor probe `all $x: person($x) & ~home($x) -> prisoner($x).`:
#   entitled(Adam, Bread, event { home() }).        control LOADS  (inert)
#   entitled(some person, Bread, event { home() }). control LOADS  (inert)
#   entitled(every person, Bread, event { home() }).control REFUSED (widened)
#   all $p: person($p) -> entitled($p, Bread, event { home() }).
#                                                   control REFUSED (widened)
#   owns(every person, event { home() }).           control REFUSED (widened)
# So ground facts and `some` are harmless; `every` and explicit rules are
# not, at ANY place, on ANY predicate — including a template-referenced
# x2. `flatten_consequent` descends the head's `And` unconditionally, so
# every abstraction body in a rule head contributes its P -> person edge.
#
# AND IT CANNOT BE BANNED, because the widening IS the firewall — both
# come from that same unconditional descent. Remove one and you remove
# the other. There is no compile-time check that keeps the protection and
# forbids the smuggle.
#
# THE GUARANTEE IS THEREFORE A TEST, NOT A RULE: complement pins. For each
# non-floor predicate this file relies on, pin that
#     all $x: person($x) & ~P($x) -> prisoner($x).
# still LOADS. That asserts the protected set is EXACTLY the floor, and it
# catches a widening from any predicate, any place, any spelling — which
# is strictly stronger than an argument check could be. Verified set, all
# currently LOADS:  home, choose, permits, false
# EXCLUDE `travel`: it is REFUSED, legitimately — Article 2 derives it as
# `person($x) & ~prisoner($x) -> travel($x)`, so travel is already inside
# the prisoner cone and a control on it is a permanent false positive.
# Any predicate appearing under negation in a rule reaching `prisoner` is
# disqualified as a control for the same reason.
#
# Keep x3 empty anyway as a local convention — x2 is the intended slot and
# a second abstraction has no business on a floor line. Editor check:
#     grep -c '^entitled(.*,.*,' this-file      must stay 0
# But understand it as housekeeping, not the guarantee.
#
# CORRECTION to an earlier claim in this file: a smuggled floor line does
# NOT render identically. A three-place floor back-translates with an
# extra scaffold conjunct — "…then Y is an event, Z is an event, and X is
# entitled to Y." It does not name the smuggled predicate, so it is
# cryptic rather than absent; "invisible" was too strong.
#
# FRAGILITY — still the largest single risk to this design, now pinned.
# The asymmetry above is an emergent property of two functions disagreeing
# about abstraction opacity. Anyone "fixing" `flatten_consequent` for
# opacity symmetry would silently make this floor stratifiable — rules
# could then punish people for lacking a floor right. As of 2026-07-28
# five regression tests pin it upstream, at
# nibli-engine/tests/integration.rs:3475-3571 (the citation read :3228
# until 2026-07-31, which is a comment banner, not the tests):
#   rights_floor_entitled_asserts_and_routes
#   rights_floor_does_not_fabricate_the_actuality   entitled TRUE, eats FALSE
#   rights_floor_blocks_punishment_for_lacking_it   names prisoner -> eats
#   punishment_rule_alone_is_stratifiable           the negative control
#   rights_floor_protection_is_placement_dependent  x2 loses it, and jails
# If those ever go red or are deleted, this article's central claim is
# gone and the book must not print the firewall argument.
#
# [v0.3] PREDICATE: `entitled` — RESOLVED, was `obligated`. The corpus
# entry was added upstream for this floor:
#   name "entitled", source_gismu "zzright1", places
#   ["holder", "entitlement", "standard"], template "{x1} is entitled to
#   {x2}", tier Curated.
# x1 is the HOLDER of the claim, which is the reading a rights floor
# needs. Rejected alternatives, each for a different reason, recorded so
# nobody re-runs the analysis:
#   `obligated_by` — `bilga`, places duty/do/standard, AND registered as
#     the x1<->x2 conversion of `obliged`, so obligated_by(A,B) compiles to
#     obliged(B,A). Written positionally by the shipped corpora, so the
#     old eight lines routed each person into a place labelled `duty` and
#     each event into one labelled `do`. Not merely inverted — garbled.
#   `deserve` — `jerna` is an EARNING predicate: x1 earns, x2 is the
#     WAGES. The floor would have put an event into the wages place, and
#     leaving x3 unfilled neutralises nothing. Generic tier, places never
#     hand-verified.
#   `permitted` — structurally excluded, and elegantly so: the floor would
#     close permits -> person -> prisoner ->(neg) permits against Article
#     6's `~permits(Appeals, ·)`, and the whole file fails to load. The
#     mechanism that protects the floor is what rules this one out.
#   `owe` — the owed party is x3, not x1. `grant` — reserved by nibli-auth.
# ARITY NOTE: 3, not the 2 originally requested, and the mitigation
# offered upstream — "the template has two placeholders, so an unfilled
# place is invisible" — is exactly what makes INVARIANT 3 necessary: a
# FILLED place is invisible too. See INVARIANT 3 above; x3 stays empty.
# RENDER LIMITATION, pre-existing and NOT introduced here: back-translating
# a floor line still leaks event scaffolding — "…then Y is an event and X
# is entitled to Y". The collapse that reads `obligated` nicely is gated on
# a hardcoded `matches!(rel, "obligated_by" | "obliged")` in
# nibli-render/src/logic.rs:26. Do NOT simply add "entitled" to that list:
# the collapse reads the party from place 2 and `entitled` has it in place
# 1, so it would print the wrong word about the wrong argument. The fix is
# to make the collapse template-driven, which also repairs the shipped
# GDPR Art 15 rendering. Irrelevant to book-1, which never shows KR.
# Do not substitute a predicate without re-running the firewall suite.
#
# ADMISSION CRITERION (replaces the vocabulary formulation in 3-spine.md,
# which was post-hoc — it admits only dwell and expresses, and would
# exclude food): a good belongs here when its absence must never be a
# permissible ground for sanction. That is the prohibition above, not a
# value claim. What the floor is NOT is justified: the formalism makes
# this commitment precise and unretractable, never right.
entitled(every person, event { secure() }).
entitled(every person, event { eats() }).
entitled(every person, event { dwell() }).
entitled(every person, event { healthy() }).
entitled(every person, event { learn() }).
entitled(every person, event { expresses() }).
# [v0.3 NEW] The inner forum. A heresy law is writable in committed
# corpus words with no lexicon extension; this line is what refuses it.
entitled(every person, event { believe() }).
# [v0.3 NEW] Company. Without it a regime could hold every convicted
# person in permanent solitary and "punishment deprives exactly one
# thing: movement" would remain literally true — the vocabulary had no
# predicate for a second human being.
entitled(every person, event { meets() }).
# [v0.9 2026-08-02 RULED] THE FIRST DELIVERY ROUTE. hears (tirna: the
# student heard the teacher) is an ARRIVAL record — two-sided, the
# teaching given and received, rating nobody's capability — and from it
# a floor actuality derives: the only route to learn, and the first
# route to any floor right that does not run through a prison. The
# residual-trust-base-II test passes by construction: the arrival
# evidence upstream of this head is the hears entry, never the roster.
# The entitlement above stays unconditional — being owed learning never
# waits on this rule; only the record's knowledge of ARRIVAL does. The
# route ships DORMANT: no arrival is recorded in the cast, the same
# posture as the placement marker — dormant is not blind, and the pins
# force it. Recognition is untouched in both directions: being taught
# still mints nothing (the grades ruling), and this rule reads teaches
# beside hears, never teaches beside itself.
# [RULED same day] This route is also the book's WHOLE delivery story:
# further routes to the other floor actualities belong to the
# operational layer (book-2), each needing its own genuinely two-sided
# arrival record and priced as the widening it is. One route
# demonstrates the shape — delivery as a rule, not a promise — and the
# destination book needs nothing more from it.
all $t: all $s: teaches($t, $s) & hears($s, $t) -> learn($s).
# <UNIVERSAL-STANDING-RULES-BEGIN>
# [v1.1] Standing starts from the supplied encounter, not a successful lookup.
# The public guard refuses a known public body passed as the subject. These rules
# are monotone: a later correction cannot retract personhood. They authenticate
# no civil identity and add no operation or delivery claim.
all $child: born($child) & ~public($child) -> person($child).
all $subject: at($subject, FirstContact) & ~public($subject) -> person($subject).
all $subject: at($subject, RepublicJurisdiction) & ~public($subject) -> person($subject).
all $subject: at($subject, EffectiveControl) & ~public($subject) -> person($subject).
