# ─── The world in facts: cast ───────────────── [v0.1, two swaps]
# [v0.3 REPLACES prisoner(Adam)] Adam was the one asserted prisoner, a fixture
# rather than a conviction, which kept `prisoner` open to assertion. He is now
# convicted through Article 6 like every other prisoner in the cast.
person(Adam).
injure(Adam, Ivo).
judge(Court, Adam).
person(Bela).
person(Cira).
teaches(Bela, Cira).
person(Dev).
person(Esa).
parent(Dev, Esa).
teaches(Esa, Fin).
judge(Dev, Esa).
person(Gia).
person(Hex).
# [v0.2 REPLACES permits(Review, Gia/Hex)] — seating now flows through
# Art 8; the credential derives because credibility is clean.
choose(Electorate, Gia).
# [v0.7] Hex is seated by the Convocation, not the Electorate. Gia and Hex
# still void Bela, but now as a cross-body pair — which is the whole point:
# the shipped case that demonstrates the rule must actually exercise it.
choose(Convocation, Hex).
judge(Gia, Bela).
capture(Gia, Bela).
judge(Hex, Bela).
capture(Hex, Bela).
authorized(Gia, IndependentReviewer, Bela).
authorized(Hex, IndependentReviewer, Bela).
# [v0.9] The examinations' recorded grounds — one complaint, two examiners.
cite(Gia, Complaint, Bela).
cite(Hex, Complaint, Bela).
person(Koa).
capture(Koa, Esa).
person(Quin).
work(Quin, Census).
person(Hano).
person(Ivo).
injure(Hano, Ivo).
judge(Court, Hano).
home(Hano).
at(Hano, PlacementHome).
# [v0.5] Hano carries EXACTLY ONE dimension, and that is the point of the
# fact. With no dimension at all `? severe(Hano). => FALSE` passes because
# there is nothing to test; with one, it is the only pin in the suite that
# fails if the two-of-three threshold ever slips to one-of-three.
attack(Hano, Ivo).
person(Jala).
injure(Jala, Ivo).
person(Lupo).
person(Mira).
teaches(Mira, Cira).
judge(Lupo, Mira).
capture(Lupo, Mira).
deceive(Lupo, Mira).
judge(Review, Lupo).
person(Nia).
injure(Nia, Ivo).
judge(Court, Nia).
clear(Nia).
# [v0.9] The generic Appeals judgment that satisfies Nia's relief rule.
# The story treats it as an examination of Nia's case; judge/2 cannot encode
# that purpose-bound link. Without this line the conjunct would strip the
# cast's one relieved person.
judge(Appeals, Nia).
person(Lalo).
person(Mina).
injure(Lalo, Mina).
judge(Court, Lalo).
home(Lalo).
at(Lalo, PlacementHome).
family(Lalo).
# [v0.5] Lalo's severity was asserted; it is now reached by the first route,
# intent and kind of harm on the same act.
attack(Lalo, Mina).
cruel(Lalo, Mina).
person(Nando).
person(Opal).
injure(Nando, Opal).
judge(Court, Nando).
family(Nando).
# [v1.4] Supplied age evidence reaches the common adulthood tier. These facts
# do not constitute adulthood by themselves; the rules above attach the status protection and franchise automatically.
at(Hano, GeneralAdulthood).
at(Jala, GeneralAdulthood).
at(Bela, GeneralAdulthood).
at(Adam, GeneralAdulthood).

# ─── Adversarial scenario: capture attempt ───── [v0.1, one swap]
person(Boss).
person(Rebel).
person(Scapegoat).
# [v0.2 REPLACES permits(Review, Boss)] Boss was seated — so Boss stays
# forever an authority (Rebel's shield survives the recall) — but broken
# blocks the credential derivation, so Boss holds no voiding power.
choose(Electorate, Boss).
judge(Boss, Rebel).
capture(Boss, Rebel).
injure(Rebel, Scapegoat).
judge(Court, Rebel).
show(Rebel, Boss).
broken(Boss).

# ─── Adversarial scenario v0.2: the three exploits, pinned ────────
# E1a — Don frames himself a whistleblower on his own VICTIM.
#       v0.1: unjailable. v0.2: Pax is no authority → no shield.
person(Don).
person(Pax).
injure(Don, Pax).
judge(Court, Don).
show(Don, Pax).
# [v0.5] Don exercises the third route — kind of harm plus a second victim —
# and he is here because without him that rule would compile and derive
# nothing, which is the failure this file is least able to see. It is not
# free: Don was one of the three convicted people the placement machinery
# housed nowhere, and severity now routes him to a facility and delivers
# `dwell`. The homeless-convict gap narrows to Kel and Adam. That is a
# consequence of putting a rule to work, not a repair of the gap.
cruel(Don, Pax).
injure(Don, Ivo).
# E1b — Sly shows a REAL authority, no Review finding yet: shield holds.
#       The disclosed fail-open window, working as chosen.
person(Sly).
injure(Sly, Pax).
judge(Court, Sly).
show(Sly, Court).
# E1c — Kel did the same, but Review adjudicated the exposure as deceit:
#       shield falls, prison holds, and the counter-audit voids Kel.
person(Kel).
injure(Kel, Pax).
judge(Court, Kel).
show(Kel, Court).
deceive(Kel, Court).
judge(Review, Kel).
# E1d — Rex did what Kel did AND exposed a second authority. The shield rule
#       is satisfied by ANY qualifying pair, so the deceit finding against the
#       first exposure does not reach the second: `defend(Rex)` holds on the
#       Review exposure while `false(Rex)` is TRUE from the counter-audit.
#       VOIDED AND SHIELDED AT ONCE. Kel closes only because Kel exposed once,
#       so the three per-subject Kel pins could never have caught this — the
#       defect is per-PAIR. This is the disclosed cost of scoping the shield to
#       the exposure rather than the person; see chapter 4. Do not "fix" it by
#       appending ~false($w) to Article 7 without re-reading that section: it
#       compiles, and it hands two cross-body auditors a route to strip the
#       shield off the whistleblower who exposed them.
#       Sena is a FRESH constant, not an existing victim: every argument in a
#       new fact must be new (except institution constants), because a new
#       person naming existing ones can complete Art 4's multi-sig.
person(Rex).
injure(Rex, Sena).
judge(Court, Rex).
show(Rex, Court).
deceive(Rex, Court).
judge(Review, Rex).
show(Rex, Review).
person(Sena).
# E2 — Vex was voided LAST epoch (rotten ledger), is still seated on
#      paper, and pairs with clean Wren against Tyr. v0.1: Tyr falls.
#      v0.2: Vex's credential never derives; Tyr stands.
person(Tyr).
person(Vex).
person(Wren).
rotten(Vex).
choose(Electorate, Vex).
# [v0.7] Wren moves to the Convocation too, and this move is LOAD-BEARING
# rather than cosmetic. E2 pairs carried-void Vex with clean Wren against
# Tyr, and `? false(Tyr). => FALSE` is supposed to prove that a prior-epoch
# void denies the credential. Leave Wren on the Electorate and the pair now
# fails on body-difference BEFORE the rotten test is ever reached — the pin
# stays green while testing nothing. Verified both ways: with Wren moved,
# deleting rotten(Vex) flips false(Tyr) TRUE; with Wren left behind, it does
# not move, which is the vacuous reading.
choose(Convocation, Wren).
judge(Vex, Tyr).
capture(Vex, Tyr).
judge(Wren, Tyr).
capture(Wren, Tyr).
cite(Vex, Notice, Tyr).
cite(Wren, Notice, Tyr).
# E3 — Ruk: severe, NON-domestic, home fact asserted (the farmhouse).
#      v0.1: soft cage, no HighSec. v0.2: HighSec routes, and the home
#      assertion itself derives a placement breach.
person(Ruk).
injure(Ruk, Opal).
judge(Court, Ruk).
# [v0.5] Ruk reaches severity by the second route: intent, plus a second
# victim recorded separately. The farmhouse case is now also the case that
# demonstrates plurality, and neither victim fact says how many there were.
attack(Ruk, Opal).
injure(Ruk, Pax).
home(Ruk).
at(Ruk, PlacementHome).
# Amendment labels — the proposals declaring the floor and register targets
# do not derive become regardless of tally; the ordinary label verdict does.
# No line here says what source effect any proposal would have.
suggest(Assembly, Amend_Floor).
adjust(Amend_Floor, Art_Floor).
ratifies(Electorate, Amend_Floor).
suggest(Assembly, Amend_Mint).
adjust(Amend_Mint, Art_Mint).
ratifies(Electorate, Amend_Mint).
suggest(Assembly, Amend_Meta).
adjust(Amend_Meta, Art_Entrench).
ratifies(Electorate, Amend_Meta).

# ─── Adversarial pins v0.3: the floor firewall ────────────────────
# POSITIVE pins — queryable, and they belong in the pinned-verdict file:
#   ? decide(Hano, Ballot).   TRUE   convicted, franchise retained
#   ? decide(Jala, Ballot).   TRUE   free, franchise held
#   ? travel(Hano).           FALSE  the one thing conviction takes
#   ? err(Hano, Isolation).   TRUE   no meets fact => breach is visible
#
# NEGATIVE pins — these cannot be expressed as queries, because the
# assertion itself must FAIL. They are the firewall's only test, and a
# harness has to run them as "this file must refuse to load":
#   all $x: person($x) & ~believe($x) -> prisoner($x).   MUST be refused
#   all $x: person($x) & ~meets($x)   -> prisoner($x).   MUST be refused
#   all $x: person($x) & ~home($x)    -> prisoner($x).   MUST load (control:
#                                       home is not a floor right, so a
#                                       refusal here would mean the test
#                                       is measuring the rule shape, not
#                                       the floor line)
# Keeping the control is the point. Without it a green firewall test
# proves nothing.

# ─── Technical appendix delta (v0.2 proxy vocabulary) ─────────────
# authority(X)   — X is an exposable office: an institution constant, or
#                  anyone the Electorate ever seated. Derived-only from
#                  asserted public/choose premises. Never revoked (see Art 7 note).
# choose(E, X)   — the Electorate seats X (asserted; the selection
#                  process is outside the firewall, this fact is its
#                  certificate).
# rotten(X)      — PRIOR-epoch voided credibility: the asserted carry ledger.
#                  Gates multi-sig and credential derivation; re-derives
#                  false(X) for clawback.
# fit(X, Homestay)    — derived home-confinement eligibility.
# err(X, Placement)   — derived placement breach: asserted home without
#                       derived eligibility.
# suggest / ratifies / adjust / permanent / become(M, Law)
#                — amendment labels (Art 9). ratifies is an asserted outcome;
#                  permanent is the declared-target label register. No rule
#                  authenticates a tally, binds an effect, or applies a change.
#                  Corpus: ca'irzau.
# endorses(B, X)  — a case-bound review decision on a custody lease or
#                  disposition (T3). Asserted, never derived. Held apart from
#                  ratifies so an amendment tally and a custody review cannot
#                  be confused in the record. Corpus: fitytu'i.
# ─── v0.3 additions ───
# believe(X)     — floor right, the inner forum (Art 1). Corpus: krici.
#                  Appears in no rule body, per the floor invariant.
# meets(X)       — floor right, company (Art 1). Appears in one rule body
#                  under negation, in the Art 6 isolation marker — which
#                  is permitted because that rule's head is `err`, not
#                  `prisoner`, so it does not enter the person cone.
# decide(X, Ballot) — legal franchise status (Art 2). Derived, with direct
#                  assertion refused. Two-place deliberately: bare `decide`
#                  means deciding anything. `vote` is not in the committed
#                  corpus and `choose` is taken by Art 8 seating — and reusing it on
#                  the floor un-stratifies Art 8 (see the Art 1 invariant).
# at(X, GeneralAdulthood) - supplied typed adulthood evidence. It supports
#                  the ballot consequence and anti-denial wall only; it
#                  neither constitutes adulthood nor proves outside age.
# err(X, Isolation) — derived isolation breach: a prisoner with no
#                  company fact.
# Verbatim-preserved v0.1 vocabulary: see utopia.nibli appendix.
all $a: choose(Electorate, $a) -> permits(Review, $a).
